			<div class="content" id="post-content-4838261">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlight</strong>: Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type</strong>: SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Page Layout</strong>:<br />
Containment Procedures. There will be two sections - Original Containment and Revised Containment Procedures.<br />
Description.<br />
Addendums. Consists of short emails, lab reports, and mission transcripts. Will be how the “meat” of the narrative is told by leading the reader along a trail of breadcrumbs.</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch</strong>:<br />
SCP-XXXX is a collective term for a genus of Glomeromycota (soil-borne fungi). A type of endoparasite only found in remote, humid environments within the latitudes of 23.5&#160;N(Tropic of Cancer) and 23.5&#160;S (Tropic of Capricorn), it infects organisms belonging to the order of Primates.</p>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1a refers to organisms infected with SCP-XXXX spores. Instances go through 3 stages of infection and are considered “mature” once they enter the fourth. Mature instances can reproduce SCP-XXXX by releasing spores. Has significant regenerational properties of interest to the Foundation as a result of SCP-XXXX “grafting” plant onto animal cells as it takes over the hosts’ body.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>SCP-XXXX-1b refers to cocoons of inactive 1a instances. Since 1a instances use sunlight to metabolize ATP (which are essentially walking plants made of fungi), they enter a sort of hibernation when deprived of it by encasing themselves in calcified plant matter. If left for too long become 1c instances.</p>
<p>SCP-XXXX-1c instances are large fungal umbrella shaped bodies approximately three (3) meters in height and bioluminescent in color. The majority of its surface is covered in microscopic cilia specialized in capturing fungal spores from its immediate atmosphere. Furthermore, SCP-XXXX-1c is capable of accelerating abnormal amounts of fungal growth in its vicinity, creating entirely new ecosystems of toxic bioluminescent fungi. These new environments are referred to as SCP-XXXX-1d. SCP-XXXX spores are common around 1c and throughout 1d.</p>
<p>Bioluminescent fungi in SCP-XXXX-1d emit a fruity aroma that compels the victim to consume it. This effect is exponential over long periods of time, therefore classifying 1c and 1d instances as cognitohazards. Once ingested orally, victims fall into a coma, making them susceptible to infection via SCP-XXXX-1a instances</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative</strong>:<br />
The Foundation tries to study an anomaly that could have medical benefits for personnel on the field if thoroughly understood. The site director, assigned to study the anomaly after recent failures in his own work, sees this as a chance to make his comeback. However, he, in in his pursuit to do so, unknowingly forces the anomaly through “artificial selection” (a process in which humans breed animals/plants in order to obtain offspring with certain traits), making the anomaly in general much more dangerous and difficult to contain than it originally was.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention Grabber</strong>: Humans make mistakes. We misjudge things which, ultimately, may end up making the situation worse. The Foundation is, at least partly, run by humans, and is therefore not exempt from this fact.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<br />
<em>As a courtesy to our readers on mobile devices, please collapse long posts. ~Zyn</em>
